            "number": 8,
            "type": "assignment",
            "title": "Logical Effort Path Sizing & Delay Characterization",
            "date": "08/10/2026",
            "sections": [
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "AIM",
                    "text": [
                        "For the given combinational logic circuit:",
                        "1] To determine the optimal gate sizes $x$ and $y$ using the method of Logical Effort ($x = 10$, $y = 15$).",
                        "2] To design and simulate the sized logic circuit in 180nm CMOS technology, calculate propagation delay from transient simulation, determine the theoretical delay using logical effort, and compare them."
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "TOOL",
                    "text": "Cadence Virtuoso"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "THEORY",
                    "text": [
                        "1. Path Logical Effort and Branching: In multistage combinational networks, the total path effort $F$ characterizes the total workload and is defined as the product of path logical effort $G = \\prod g_i$, path branching effort $B = \\prod b_i$, and path electrical effort $H = C_{out} / C_{in}$. Logical effort $g$ accounts for the topological complexity of each gate relative to a standard inverter, while branching effort $b$ accounts for capacitive loading on off-path fan-out branches.",
                        "2. Optimal Stage Effort and Transistor Sizing: Minimum path delay is achieved when each of the $N$ stages bears identical stage effort $\\hat{f} = F^{1/N}$. Using this optimal effort, intermediate gate sizes are uniquely determined from input to output using the stage effort relation $\\hat{f} = g_i \\cdot h_i = g_i \\cdot \\frac{C_{out,i}}{C_{in,i}}$. Transistor channel widths for each gate are then scaled proportionally to match the calculated input capacitance.",
                        "3. Path Delay Formulation: The minimum normalized path delay consists of the sum of stage effort delays and parasitic delays: $D = N \\cdot \\hat{f} + P$, where $P = \\sum p_i$ is the total path parasitic delay. The absolute theoretical propagation delay is evaluated by multiplying the normalized delay by the unit inverter delay, $T_{p,\\text{theor}} = D \\cdot t_{inv}$."
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "table",
                    "number": 4,
                    "new_page": true,
                    "title": "Tables",
                    "tables": [
                        {
                            "title": "Design Parameters",
                            "csv": "param.csv"
                        },
